%I A402395 #9 Nov 24 2026 12:24:38
%S A402395 6,28,492,496,1652,5334,8128,18420,267834,544310,6680502
%N A402395 Numbers k such that the number of distinct sums of distinct proper divisors of k equals k.
%C A402395 Numbers k such that A193279(k) = k.
%C A402395 Every term is perfect or abundant, since A193279(k) <= sigma(k) - k.
%C A402395 All even perfect numbers are terms (see the Adamczewski link in A193279).
%C A402395 The terms that are not perfect begin 492, 1652, 5334, 18420, 267834, 544310, 6680502.
%C A402395 There are no other terms below 10^7.
%C A402395 The first 11 terms are all Zumkeller numbers (A083207). - _Ivan N. Ianakiev_, Nov 23 2026
%e A402395 492 is a term: its proper divisors 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12, 41, 82, 123, 164, 246 sum to 684, but only 492 different numbers are sums of one or more of them.
%o A402395 (Python)
%o A402395 from sympy import divisors
%o A402395 def ok(k):
%o A402395     s = 1
%o A402395     for d in divisors(k)[:-1]:
%o A402395         s |= s << d
%o A402395     return bin(s).count('1') - 1 == k
%o A402395 print([k for k in range(1, 20001) if ok(k)])
